   $q$Why do researchers choose a random sample when they want to estimate a population value?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It guarantees the sample statistic equals the population parameter","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-random-guarantees-exact"},{"text":"It allows the researchers to conclude cause and effect","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-sampling-with-assignment"},{"text":"It tends to give a sample that represents the population, so the statistic is an unbiased estimate","is_correct":true},{"text":"It removes the need for a large sample","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-random-replaces-sample-size"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It tends to give a sample that represents the population, so the statistic is an unbiased estimate$q$, $q$Random selection avoids favoring any group, so sample statistics vary around the true parameter without bias, though they still differ from it by chance. Cause and effect comes from random assignment in an experiment, not random sampling.$q$, 'draft', null)
