   $q$In a randomized experiment, plants given a new soil mix grew $2.1$ centimeters more on average than plants in regular soil. Re-randomizing the results many times gives differences that are approximately normal with mean $0$ and standard deviation $1.4$ centimeters. Using a cutoff of 2 standard deviations, which conclusion is best?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The soil mix has no effect on growth at all","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"treated-weak-evidence-as-proof-of-no-effect"},{"text":"The soil mix causes $2.1$ centimeters of extra growth","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-chance-variation"},{"text":"A difference of $2.1$ could easily come from random assignment alone, so there is not convincing evidence the soil mix helps","is_correct":true},{"text":"The study must be repeated as an observational study to draw any conclusion","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-study-types"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A difference of $2.1$ could easily come from random assignment alone, so there is not convincing evidence the soil mix helps$q$, $q$The cutoff is $2(1.4) = 2.8$, and $2.1 < 2.8$, so a difference this large happens often by chance and the result is not significant. That does not prove the mix has no effect; it only means this experiment did not show one.$q$, 'draft', null)
