   $q$Newborns at a hospital have weights with mean $3.2$ kilograms and standard deviation $0.4$ kilograms, lengths with mean $50$ centimeters and standard deviation $2$ centimeters, and head circumferences with mean $35$ centimeters and standard deviation $1.2$ centimeters. Which measurement is the most unusual for its distribution?$q$, $q$[{"text":"A weight of $2.3$ kilograms","is_correct":true},{"text":"A weight of $4.0$ kilograms","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misjudged-z-magnitude"},{"text":"A head circumference of $36$ centimeters","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-the-standard-deviation"},{"text":"A length of $52$ centimeters","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"compared-raw-differences"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A weight of $2.3$ kilograms$q$, $q$The z-scores are $\frac{2.3 - 3.2}{0.4} = -2.25$, $\frac{52 - 50}{2} = 1$, $\frac{36 - 35}{1.2} \approx 0.83$ (rounded to the nearest hundredth) and $\frac{4.0 - 3.2}{0.4} = 2$; the largest in size is $|-2.25| = 2.25$. The length of $52$ has the biggest raw difference, but it is only one standard deviation from its mean.$q$, 'draft', null)
