   $q$Two random polls about a school board race were taken a week apart. Poll 1 finds Ms. Okafor with $54\%$ support, margin of error $3$ percentage points. Poll 2 finds her with $52\%$ support, margin of error $4$ percentage points. Which statement is supported?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both polls show convincing evidence that a majority supports her","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-the-margin-of-error"},{"text":"Neither poll shows convincing evidence of majority support","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misused-the-margin-of-error"},{"text":"Only poll 2 gives convincing evidence, because its margin of error is larger","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thought-larger-margin-is-stronger"},{"text":"Poll 1 gives convincing evidence of majority support, but poll 2 does not","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Poll 1 gives convincing evidence of majority support, but poll 2 does not$q$, $q$Poll 1 gives plausible values from $51\%$ to $57\%$, all above $50\%$, while poll 2 gives $48\%$ to $56\%$, which includes values below $50\%$. Saying both show a majority ignores that poll 2's interval dips below $50\%$.$q$, 'draft', null)
